.TH MACHASH 1 "August 2026" "machash 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
machash \- hash strings into MAC addresses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B machash
.RI "[options] [string...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
machash hashes each input string with the 48-bit Bobcat hash and
prints the result as a colon-separated MAC address:
.PP
.EX
$ machash \-s hello
0f:c2:c1:58:42:59
.EE
.PP
The address is fake, but it has the form of a valid MAC address.
It is useful for deriving a stable, deterministic MAC address from
any string.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s STR, \-\-string STR
Hash
.IR STR .
You can repeat this option.
(default: none)
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-plain
Print the raw 48-bit hash as 12 lowercase hex digits.
There are no separators and no 0x prefix.
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-swap
Print the six octets in reversed order.
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-0x
Print the raw hash with a 0x prefix.
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-unicast
Clear the multicast bit so the result is always a unicast address.
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-local
Set the locally-administered bit of the result.
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-lines
Read stdin as lines. Each line is one input.
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-f FILE, \-\-file FILE
Read
.I FILE
as lines. Each line is one input.
(default: none)
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-hostname
Use the name of this host as the single input.
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-i IFACE, \-\-interface IFACE
Use the name of this host and
.IR IFACE ,
joined by a colon, as the single input.
(default: none)
.TP
.B \-\-check MAC
Print match or no match for each input, compared against
.IR MAC .
(default: off)
.TP
.B \-L LVL, \-\-loglevel LVL
Set the log level to off, fatal, error, warn, info, debug, or 0-5.
(default: warn)
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show the help text and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and build info, and exit.
.PP
The output formats (the default MAC form,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-0x ,
and
.BR \-S )
are mutually exclusive.
.B \-\-check
is a mode and cannot be combined with a format flag.
.SH INPUT
Strings come from
.B \-\-string
and from positional arguments, in the order they appear.
If you give no strings, machash reads all of stdin as one input.
Multi-line data stays a single input.
Leading and trailing whitespace is stripped before hashing.
Options are recognized only before the first positional argument.
After that, all arguments are strings.
A bare
.B \-\-
ends option parsing.
.PP
Line mode reads one input per line.
.B \-\-lines
reads the lines of stdin.
.B \-\-file
reads the lines of a file.
Line mode cannot be combined with
.B \-\-string
or positional arguments.
.PP
Hostname mode takes the input from the machine.
.B \-\-hostname
uses the host name.
.B \-\-interface
uses the host name and the interface name, joined by a colon.
Hostname mode gives one input.
It cannot be combined with other input sources, and stdin is
ignored.
.SH OUTPUT
By default, machash prints the digest as six colon-separated
lowercase hex octets, most significant octet first.
If the result is a multicast address and you did not request plain
or unicast output, machash prints a warning on stderr.
.PP
In check mode, machash prints one line per input:
.BR match
or
.BR "no match" .
The exit status is 0 if all inputs match, and 1 if any input does
not match.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status is 0 on success.
It is 1 if an error occurs, or if any input does not match in
check mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
Hash a string from a flag and from stdin:
.PP
.EX
$ machash \-s hello
0f:c2:c1:58:42:59
$ echo hello | machash
0f:c2:c1:58:42:59
.EE
.PP
Print the raw hash, with the multicast bit cleared:
.PP
.EX
$ machash \-pu \-s hello
0fc2c1584259
.EE
.PP
Hash each line of a file:
.PP
.EX
$ machash \-f names.txt
.EE
.PP
Derive one stable MAC address for this host:
.PP
.EX
$ machash \-n
7f:b8:8e:31:f2:d8
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
The project page, with the documentation for the Bobcat hash, the
output formats, check mode, and hostname mode:
.PP
.RI https://github.com/MrDrMcCoy/machash
.SH VERSION
This man page documents machash 1.0.1.
